{:edb/db-before-t 1634 :edb/db-after-t 1635 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534947 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:46:49.302Z" 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186268907 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(4,2)$\n- ${f}_{x}(0,2)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(0,2)$ is defined" 13194139534947 17592186045417 false] [17592186268907 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(4,2)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(0,2)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{y}(0,2)$ is defined" 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186382207 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{z}$ is defined only when $xy\\ge 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{y{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2z\\sqrt{xy}}$ is defined only when $xy>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${f}_{y}=\\frac{x{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2z\\sqrt{xy}}$ is the same\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{z}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{{z}^{2}}$ is defined only when $xy\\ge 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186382207 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{z}$ is defined only when $xy\\ge 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{y{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2z\\sqrt{xy}}$ is defined only when $xy>0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${f}_{y}=\\frac{x{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2z\\sqrt{xy}}$ is the same\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{z}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{{z}^{2}}$ is defined only when $xy\\ge 0$ and $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy\\ge 0,z\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0,z\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534947 17592186045417 false] [17592186382208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382209 13194139534947 17592186045417 false] [17592186382208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382210 13194139534947 17592186045417 false] [17592186382208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382211 13194139534947 17592186045417 false] [17592186382208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382212 13194139534947 17592186045417 false] [17592186382208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382213 13194139534947 17592186045417 false] [17592186382208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445248 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186382208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445249 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186382208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445250 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186382208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445252 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186382208 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445254 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186382208 1078 #edb/ref 17592186382210 13194139534947 17592186045417 false] [17592186382208 1078 #edb/ref 17592186445252 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186382221 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z}?$ \n\n- ${f}_{y}(1,1,1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{z}(0,1,2)$ is defined\n- The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,1,1)$" 13194139534947 17592186045417 false] [17592186382221 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z}?$ \n\nI. ${f}_{y}(1,1,1)$ is defined\n\nII. ${f}_{z}(0,1,2)$ is defined\n\nIII. The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,1,1)$" 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186409555 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409539 13194139534947 17592186045417 false] [17592186409555 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409540 13194139534947 17592186045417 false] [17592186409555 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409545 13194139534947 17592186045417 false] [17592186409555 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409547 13194139534947 17592186045417 false] [17592186409555 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409552 13194139534947 17592186045417 false] [17592186409555 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445247 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186409555 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445251 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186409555 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445253 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186409555 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445255 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186409555 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445256 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186409555 1078 #edb/ref 17592186409539 13194139534947 17592186045417 false] [17592186409555 1078 #edb/ref 17592186445251 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186409559 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ is defined only when $xyz>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z}$ is defined only when $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186409559 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ is defined only when $xyz>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z}$ is defined only when $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534947 17592186045417 false] [17592186445246 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445257 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186445246 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445258 13194139534947 17592186045417 true] [17592186445246 1043 #edb/ref 17592186445259